==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|amuse|er}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Someone who [[amuse]]s.
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} One who [[divert]]s [[attention]], usually to [[distract]] or [[bewilder]], often for [[fraud]]ulent purposes; hence a [[cheat]], [[deceive]]r or [[thief]].
# {{rfv-sense}} {{context|1811|lang=en}} One of a class of rogues who carry snuff or dust in their pockets, which they throw into the eyes of people so as to enable their accomplices to rob them while pretending to help them.

===Anagrams===
* [[Maseru#English|Maseru]], [[Mauser#English|Mauser]]

----

==French==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|fr}} {{term|lang=frm|amuser||to amuse, divert, babble}}, from {{etyl|fro|fr}} {{term|amuser|lang=fro||to stupefy, waste time, be lost in thought}}, from {{term|a-|lang=fro}} + {{term|lang=fro|muser||to stare stupidly at, gape, wander, waste time, loiter, think carefully about, attend to}}, of uncertain and obscure origin. Cognate with {{etyl|oc|-}} {{term|musa||idle waiting|lang=oc}}, {{etyl|it|-}} {{term|musare||to gape idly about|lang=it}}. Possibly from {{etyl|fro|-}} {{term/t|fro|*mus||snout}} from Proto-{{etyl|roa|-}} {{term/t|la|*mūsa||snout}} (—compare {{etyl|ML.|fr}} {{term/t|la|mūsum||muzzle, snout}}), from {{etyl|gem-pro|fr}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*mū-||muzzle, snout}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|fr}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*mū-||lips, muzzle}}. Compare {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Maul||muzzle, snout|lang=de}}.

Alternative etymology connects {{etyl|fro|-}} ''muser'' and {{etyl|oc|-}} ''musa'' with {{etyl|goh|fr}} {{term|muoza||careful attention, leisure, idleness|lang=frk}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|fr}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*mōtǭ||leave, permission}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|fr}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*med-||to acquire, possess, control}}. Compare also {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|muozon|muozōn|to be idle, have leisure or opportunity|lang=goh}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Musse||leisure|lang=de}}. More at {{l/en|empty}}.

